      "recommended_risk": "low",
      "technical_explanation": "The scanner raised a low-confidence obfuscation signal at the first line of this extension entrypoint, but the supplied full source contradicts any obfuscation finding. The file is a plainly readable, heavily commented ES module following standard SillyTavern extension conventions: it validates the host context, registers generation-ended and chat-changed event handlers for continuity extraction, registers user-facing slash commands, mounts a settings panel through the host template API, and exposes named bridge helpers on the global object. It performs no dynamic code evaluation, contains no encoded or packed payloads, and makes no network requests within this file. Data movement is limited to local clipboard writes, a user-initiated local JSON download and import, and host settings persistence, all proportionate to the stated continuity-memo purpose. The generation interceptor installation is a disclosed capability consistent with the project description. The most plausible trigger for the heuristic is the decorative non-ASCII box-drawing characters used in comment banners, which is scanner noise rather than concealed behavior.",
      "layman_explanation": "An automated scanner guessed the code might be deliberately hidden or scrambled, but the actual file is ordinary, clearly written, well-commented code. It does exactly what a SillyTavern continuity-notes extension should do, its features are visible to the user, and nothing in it hides its behavior or sends data anywhere unexpected. The warning appears to be a false alarm, likely triggered by decorative divider lines drawn with special characters inside the comments.",
